Welcome to the {{ group.name }}, and thank you for joining!

The {{ group.name }} was established in 2018, in response to the growing role of machine learning–based AI in technology. At the time, attention was turning to the "black box" nature of AI systems and the challenges of explainability, transparency and replicability. Since then, modelling AI agents and protocols has become a priority for participants.

Knowledge Representation (KR) was seen from the start as a way to make AI systems easier to explain and to examine. The aim is to produce artefacts and methods for the shared representation of AI: vocabularies, ontologies, taxonomies, notes and specifications.

AI has kept evolving. Large language models now serve as natural language interfaces to AI tools, applications and environments, and AI agents act on behalf of people and organisations. The group's work follows these changes and looks at how KR can support interoperability, explainability, structured prompting and trustworthy AI in these dynamic environments.

Current focus: knowledge representation of trust. Work so far addresses the agentic layer, where trust is chained across agents that verify one another's identity and authority. Trust in non-agentic AI, including machine learning models and LLMs, is open for contributions:
  https://github.com/w3c-cg/aikr/blob/main/trust/trustinai(non_agentic).md

